Which sampling frequencies represented the 7 kHz signal correctly?
24 kHz and 16 kHz. Both satisfy the Nyquist criterion (fs >= 14 kHz), preserving the original 7 kHz frequency without distortion.

When did the 7 kHz signal appear as another frequency?
At 12 kHz and 8 kHz. At these rates, the sampling frequency dropped below the required Nyquist limit (fs < 14 kHz).

What happened when the Nyquist frequency became lower than 7 kHz?
Aliasing occurred. The 7 kHz signal folded back into the fundamental Nyquist range [0, fs/2], appearing as false lower frequencies (5 kHz and 1 kHz).

Did the aliased signal sound different?
Yes. The audio pitch dropped significantly from a high-pitched 7 kHz tone down to 5 kHz (at fs = 12 kHz) and 1 kHz (at fs = 8 kHz).

Why can MATLAB not recover the original 7 kHz signal after aliasing?
Information is permanently lost. Downsampling without an anti-aliasing filter causes the high-frequency samples to overlap with lower-frequency ones, making them mathematically indistinguishable.
